  "summary": "Overnight markets are digesting a Democratic midterm sweep with cheaper oil and a bid for bonds. Brent is 94.62 and WTI is 88.12, a sharp break from the prior three-digit handle, while gold is 4073, the S&P holds near 7569, and the VIX is 20.83. Official SOFR printed 3.97 on 3 November; the strip still prices hikes at December 4.165, March 4.36, and September 4.505, so cash-versus-March leftover is about 39 basis points. Canada leftover is similar, with cash CORRA 2.53 versus March 2.93. Dollar-yen sits at 158.25, Australian-kiwi remains elevated at 1.2445, and kiwi-dollar is still washed out near 0.5637. Watch whether cheaper crude fades the Canada and Australia hike paths, whether the post-election bond bid extends into the jobs print, and whether crowded Australian-kiwi mean-reverts without a New Zealand policy shock.",
  "type": "OVERNIGHT_ACCEPTED_PUBLIC_NEWS"
